1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:00,360
So,

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:02,070
um,

3
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:06,189
what happens when a massive government roadmap gets approved?

4
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:12,880
I mean, in your ministry, you know, the documents are signed, the funding is allocated, and you sort of assume the hard work is over.

5
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,000
Right. You think you just have to execute the plan.

6
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:15,920
Exactly.

7
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:22,330
But then you have to actually implement those directives across your program, and you realize pretty quickly that an approved document,

8
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,320
well, it doesn't automatically change how systems get built.

9
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:31,760
So welcome to today's deep dive. We're looking at phase five of the National Enterprise Architecture, or EA.

10
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:35,000
Yeah, phase five. It's, uh, it's titled Execute and Govern.

11
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:42,430
And our mission today is to explore how an approved architecture roadmap becomes a permanent practice in the demonstration country of Progressa.

12
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,780
Right. So phase five is basically the phase that never ends.

13
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:49,190
It's what makes the architecture a mandatory operational standard.

14
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:56,120
And on Progressa, there are three specific things that enforce this: uh, the repository, the board, and the review gate.

15
00:00:56,480 --> 00:01:01,290
Okay, so with these massive government programs, I mean, they're always starting and stopping.

16
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:03,780
There's constant turnover with the personnel.

17
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,900
Who actually maintains the architecture when that turnover happens?

18
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:09,080
Well, that's where the repository comes in.

19
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:13,920
It goes live as the single authoritative store of Progressa's architecture.

20
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:15,440
So it's like the central record.

21
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:19,800
Exactly. It holds all the architectural layers, the sourcing decisions.

22
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:22,800
Meaning whether they build custom software or buy it.

23
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:28,580
Right, exactly. Plus, it holds the guiding principles and a formal log of all design decisions.

24
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:37,520
And to keep it from getting outdated, they have one named architect who is explicitly responsible for keeping it current as each project wave finishes.

25
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:38,180
Oh, wow,

26
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:39,600
just one person.

27
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:44,460
Well, one person updating the log, but alongside them, Progressa sets up a permanent EA team.

28
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:48,980
Okay, if they're overseeing an entire country's systems, how big is this team?

29
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:49,820
It must be huge.

30
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:53,120
Actually, no. It requires only two to four architects.

31
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:55,280
Wait, really? Just two to four?

32
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:58,890
Yeah, just two to four. They report directly to the digitalization officer.

33
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:06,580
They serve as the permanent authority for cross-cutting decisions, and, you know, they exist whether or not any single project is currently active.

34
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:09,949
I do have to push back a bit here on how this works in reality.

35
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:13,860
Like, say I'm running a well-funded scholarship program in your ministry.

36
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:18,040
Okay, sure. And I'm on a really tight legislative deadline.

37
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:22,780
I'm not gonna wait for an external team to help me integrate with the existing learner registry.

38
00:02:23,210 --> 00:02:25,140
I'm just gonna build my own small learner list.

39
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:27,090
Because it's faster for you. Right. Yeah.

40
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:33,000
It lets me launch on time. Yeah. For a rushed project, isn't building an independent list a completely rational choice?

41
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:36,880
I mean, for your specific project, yes, it's a highly rational choice.

42
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:41,880
You're optimizing for your deadline. But that's exactly what the review gate is designed to catch.

43
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:44,850
Oh, so the review gate steps in before you can even build it.

44
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:45,810
Exactly. Yeah.

45
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:53,700
Before the scholarship program gets technical approval or starts development, the review gate evaluates the design against the repository.

46
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:56,780
It operates as a mandatory funding checkpoint.

47
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:57,780
And what are they looking for?

48
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:00,750
The review gate asks one defining question:

49
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,930
Does a shared building block already exist for this function?

50
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:10,760
And the repository will confirm that, yes, the learner registry was established and made authoritative back in wave one.

51
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:17,190
But what if the gate is bypassed? Like, what happens to the country's systems if the program just builds its own list anyway?

52
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:20,720
If that happens, Progressa's systems would become severely fragmented.

53
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:24,240
Fragmented how? Well, the learner list would be duplicated.

54
00:03:24,540 --> 00:03:33,380
You'd have educational data sitting in totally disconnected databases, which increases the cost to maintain them and makes data synchronization impossible.

55
00:03:33,390 --> 00:03:34,700
So how do you actually stop it?

56
00:03:34,710 --> 00:03:42,520
The board steps in. The board reviews the gate's findings and issues a binding ruling that the project must consume the existing learner registry.

57
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:46,640
They explicitly direct the program not to build a fourth list.

58
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:50,160
Couldn't a standard procurement rule just stop them from buying the software?

59
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:55,420
No, because procurement evaluates budgets and vendor contracts.

60
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:57,800
They lack a whole-of-government architectural view.

61
00:03:58,040 --> 00:03:58,960
Ah, I see.

62
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:08,220
To enforce this, you really need a board that has both the comprehensive system data and the institutional authority to halt a redundant project.

63
00:04:08,790 --> 00:04:13,400
Their ruling goes directly into the decision log with the technical justification.

64
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:14,100
That makes sense.

65
00:04:14,390 --> 00:04:22,320
And what's really interesting is that four times a year, this board convenes the business side and the IT side over the exact same repository.

66
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:25,900
So they're evaluating the same data to make operational decisions together.

67
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:31,760
Right. They evaluate the repository data to align business goals with technical constraints.

68
00:04:31,870 --> 00:04:39,520
This ensures both sides are coordinating future waves of development based on shared technical facts rather than just isolated department goals.

69
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,700
Okay, so to summarize phase five on Progressa,

70
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:45,900
the repository operates as a live single source of truth.

71
00:04:46,530 --> 00:04:49,260
The board issues binding decisions based on it.

72
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:52,620
The review gate prevents duplicated lists before they get funded.

73
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:57,400
And finally, the IT and business sides use this structure to make joint decisions quarterly forever.

